{"number": 91, "text": "                       Finding         out     or    making         sure?\n                       Questions            (1)\n\n                   C70\n                       When   we ask a question, we might be trying to find out information that we don’t already\n                       know.  Alternatively, we might ask a question in order to make sure that information we think\n                       we  know  is, in fact, correct.\n\n                       Finding out questions usually end with a falling tone:\n                              WHAT    part of SPAIN were  you in              HOW    much  ARE  they\n\n                       Note: Because wh- questions are often used to find out information, they often, although not always,\n                       end with a falling tone.\n\n                       Making  sure questions usually end with a rising or fall-rising tone (for the difference see B):\n                              was BRIan  there @?   or: was  BRlan  there 4}? &\n                              DOESn’t  she WORK    with you  @?    or: DOESn’t  she WORK    with you\n\n                       Note: Because yes-no questions are often used to make sure, they often, although not always, end with\n                       a rising or fall-rising tone.\n\n                       However,  wh-  questions can have a rising or fall-rising tone when they are making sure, and\n                       yes—no question can  have a falling tone when they are finding out:\n                              WHEN’S    your birthday @?\n                                                                          a rising tone shows that I’m checking the\n                                                                          date; it might be polite to suggest that I do\n                                                                          know but have temporarily forgotten\n\n\n                              Have you  SEEN  her recently &@?            I don’t know whether you have or not )\n                   C71\n                       In making  sure questions we can usually use a\n                       fall-rising tone or a rising tone with little difference\n                       in meaning. However,  a fall-rising tone often sounds\n                       more  polite than a rising tone. In particular, a\n                       fall-rising tone is often preferred in questions asked\n                       for social reasons; that is, mainly to be polite and\n                       friendly rather than to check information (see also\n                                                                                                   Are  you sure you\n                       Unit 48C):\n                                                                                                   can  manage that?\n                              bo you  WANT   to take your COAT   off\n                              Are you SURE   you can MANage    that #4)? &\n\n\n                       Other  kinds of questions can also be used to produce a reply from a hearer. In these, too, we use\n                       a falling tone to find out and a rising (or fall-rising) tone to make sure. For example, sentences\n                       which  ask for assistance with would you mind, perhaps  or I wonder usually have a falling tone:\n                              would  you MIND   holding  THIS               |          if you could HELP  me\n\n                       Note: Wh-  and yes-no questions used to offer assistance often have a falling tone as this sounds more\n                       genuine, and therefore more polite, than a rising or fall-rising tone (see also Unit 48C):\n                                   HELP you                 WHAT  can     for you\n\n                       Statements which  are intended to produce a reply often have a rising tone because they are\n                       usually asking for confirmation of something we  think we already know:\n                              You've FINished  alREADy   @?           you HAVen’t  even STARted\n\n              92\n                          English Pronunciation in Use (Advanced)", "char_count": 4145, "mean_conf": 91.72, "word_count": 467, "words": [{"text": "C70", "x0": 359, "y0": 342, "x1": 412, "y1": 366, "conf": 94, "bold": false}, {"text": "C71", "x0": 358, "y0": 1728, "x1": 410, "y1": 1751, "conf": 96, "bold": false}, {"text": "92", "x0": 264, "y0": 3190, "x1": 309, "y1": 3222, "conf": 96, "bold": false}, {"text": "Finding", "x0": 451, "y0": 109, "x1": 718, "y1": 193, "conf": 96, "bold": false}, {"text": "out", "x0": 755, "y0": 114, "x1": 877, "y1": 173, "conf": 96, "bold": false}, {"text": "or", "x0": 909, "y0": 128, "x1": 981, "y1": 173, "conf": 95, "bold": false}, {"text": "making", "x0": 1016, "y0": 109, "x1": 1281, "y1": 193, "conf": 96, "bold": false}, {"text": "sure?", "x0": 1319, "y0": 109, "x1": 1508, "y1": 173, "conf": 96, "bold": false}, {"text": "Questions", "x0": 450, "y0": 202, "x1": 810, "y1": 267, "conf": 95, "bold": false}, {"text": "(1)", "x0": 848, "y0": 199, "x1": 941, "y1": 281, "conf": 96, "bold": false}, {"text": "When", "x0": 447, "y0": 346, "x1": 555, "y1": 381, "conf": 96, "bold": false}, {"text": "we", "x0": 572, "y0": 359, "x1": 623, "y1": 381, "conf": 96, "bold": false}, {"text": "ask", "x0": 641, "y0": 346, "x1": 698, "y1": 381, "conf": 96, "bold": false}, {"text": "a", "x0": 717, "y0": 359, "x1": 735, "y1": 381, "conf": 95, "bold": false}, {"text": "question,", "x0": 751, "y0": 348, "x1": 920, "y1": 391, "conf": 95, "bold": false}, {"text": "we", "x0": 937, "y0": 359, "x1": 989, "y1": 381, "conf": 96, "bold": false}, {"text": "might", "x0": 1006, "y0": 346, "x1": 1111, "y1": 391, "conf": 96, "bold": false}, {"text": "be", "x0": 1128, "y0": 346, "x1": 1168, "y1": 381, "conf": 96, "bold": false}, {"text": "trying", "x0": 1186, "y0": 348, "x1": 1291, "y1": 391, "conf": 95, "bold": fa